        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Chibis Lower Body Negative Pressure device provides a controlled, measured orthostatic stress to the cardiovascular system.  Built in Russia, it was first used on Salyut-1.
<P>
The crewmember dons the suit by placing both legs inside the device with a pressure seal at the iliac crest level.  A microprocessor creates a variable partial vacuum on the lower half of the body.  Rarefaction to -35 mmHg was used on early missions, and increased to -45 mmHg on later flights.  It is used routinely during long-duration flights as both a diagnostic device and as a pre-landing countermeasure to stress the cardiovascular system.
<P>
The crewmember, when in the suit, is connected to the ship’s on-board systems by an air hose and an electric line or by an electric line only.  Since the line can be of any desired length, the crewmember retains almost complete freedom of movement throughout the station; the crewmember can assume different poses, and during a decompression period, can carry out professional duties.  
<P>
The design of the suit allows it to be worn for a prolonged period of time; it is convenient to use and is well ventilated.  Due to the corrugated trousers and adjustable components on the shoulder straps, the Russians describe the Chibis as “dimensionless”.  It is put on and worn without assistance from any other person.  The suit and vacuum unit weigh 8.7 kg and 3.7 kg, respectively.
<P>
Regulation of rarefaction is accomplished by changing the ratio of the air entering and leaving during a stable operation regime of the pump.  The suit is rated for a rarefaction as great as -65 mmHg; it contains a valve that endures that the rarefaction will be greater than this intensity.  Pressure fluctuations in the suit occur during the movement of the legs.  When creating rarefaction in the suit, which is accomplished by folding the corrugated trousers and drawing in the elastic fabric, a force appears which acts on the human body.  Under the influence of this force, the part of the body in the foot-belt sector is compressed, and if over-the-shoulder straps are used, the effect is propagated to the entire backbone.  Since the lower part of the abdomen falls in the decompression zone, the intestines and stomach are drawn in the direction of the pelvis; diaphragm, heart, and lungs occupy a position characteristic for a standing posture under ground gravitation conditions.  This also facilitates evaluation of its spatial position as vertical.<END>
        </div>

